the common entrapment sites were excluded (ulnar nerve was stimulated below elbow). 
Facial movements, upper and lower limbs muscle power, 
upper and lower extremities reflexes, all sensations, and 
coordination were completely normal. 
All blood and urine tests were normal including thyroid 
hormones. Thoracic computerized tomography scan was 
also normal. His cerebrospinal fluid protein was 0.7 g/L, 
without cells. The serum antiacetylcholine receptor anti- 
body level was negative. 
Nerve conduction studies performed 3 weeks after the 
first symptoms clearly had findings of an acute or subacute 
demyelinating polyneuropathy (asymmetric and patchy 
involvement with conduction block without temporal dis- 
persion) with bilateral facial nerve proximal demyelination 
(see Table 1). Repetitive stimulation of the right ulnar 
nerve produced a normal decremental response at 3 Hz, 
but single-fiber electromyography of both orbicularis oculi 
muscles showed an increased jitter in 50% of 20 pairs inves- 
tigated. Needle sampling performed on right tibialis ante- 
rior and orbicularis oris showed no spontaneous activity, 
motor unit potentials with normal morphology, and inter- 
ference pattern on full contraction. 
The patient had a diagnosis of predominantly ocular 
mysthenia gravis before electromyography investigation, 
and he was treated with pyridostigmine 60 mg every 8 h 
with marked improvement. The patient showed rapid de- 
creasing of all symptoms and 6 weeks after the beginning 
of his clinical picture he was able to stop all medication 
without any impairment. He has been well for the last 
8 months. 
To our knowledge typical ocular myasthenic fluctuat- 
ing symptoms in demyelinating polyneuropathy was only 
described once,' in a patient with positive response to edro- 
phronium; nevertheless jitter and repetitive stimulation in- 
vestigation were not referred. The clinical picture of' our 
patient suggests typical ocular myasthenic fluctuating symp- 
toms as a variant of Guillain-Barre syndrome. 
Demyelination of peripheral nerves impairs the trans- 
mission of impulses by current leakage due to increased 
capacitance and decreased resistance resulting in impulse 
propagation fai1~r-e.~ As a consequence segmental demye- 
lination in the terminal nerve fibers might produce in- 
creased jitter as reported in multiple s~lerosis.~ 
From this observation we conclude that patients with 
recent-onset ocular myasthenia gravis symptoms should be 
submitted to careful nerve conduction studies in order to 
exclude this variant of Guillain-BarrP syndrome. 
