LOBULATED MUSCLE FIBERS HAVE A 
NORMAL CYTOSKELETAL NETWORK 
Lobulated fibers (LF) are a nonspecific change occurring 
in several long-standing neuromuscular The 
peculiar cytoarchitectural pattern is the presence of small 
subsarcolemmal triangular zones with increased NADH- 
tetrazolium reductase (NADH-TR) reaction, extending 
into the interior of the muscle fiber, which is usually type 1 
and atrophic. Ultrastructural studies show large peripheral 
aggregates of normal mitochondria, focal areas of Z-line 
streaming, and disrupted myofibrils. Increased glycogen 
amount, accumulation of sarcoplasmic reticulum, and 
basement membrane thickening have been inconstantly 
reported. It has been suggested that LF represent a mor- 
phological expression of muscle cell disintegration and 
that they may progress from LF to more atrophic core-like 
fibers5 However, the origin and the nature of LF remain 
still unclear. We report herein the results of an immunocy- 
tochemical investigation of the cytoskeleton in LF. 
We have reviewed our series of 1000 consecutive muscle 
biopsies. LF were seen in 11 cases. All were adults, aged 
19-61 years (40 ? 14; mean 2 SD), and had a variety of 
neuromuscular diseases, including limb-girdle muscular 
dystrophy (7), facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy 
(1), distal myopathy (1) , spinal muscular atrophy (l), and 
acid maltase deficiency (1). LF were present with a percent- 
age ranging from 4% to 90% of muscle fibers. Muscle 
samples stored at -60°C were used. Six-micron cryostat 
transverse sections were processed with monoclonal anti- 
bodies against sarcoplasmic (titin, desmin, vimentin) and 
sarcolemmal (C-terminal dystrophin, P-spectrin, vinculin, 
talin) cytoskeletal proteins.*,' Muscle specimens from nor- 
mal subjects and patients with different neuromuscular 
diseases, showing no LF on NADH-TR, were used as con- 
trols. 
Immunocytochemistry of cytoskeletal proteins in LF 
was similar to that in normal muscle fibers. Titin and des- 
min immunoreactivities were found throughout the cyto- 
plasm and at some parts of sarcolemma. Vimentin was 
absent in LF, but present in regenerating fibers, vessel 
walls, and fibroblasts. Dystrophin, pspectrin, vinculin, and 
talin were colocalized on the surfaces of LF and there was 
no binding of intracellular structures. 
The study of cytoskeletal proteins has recently provided 
insight into the pathogenetic mechanisms of several struc- 
tural abnormalities of muscle fibers. In acid maltase defi- 
ciency and other vacuolar myopathies, vacuoles are immu- 
nolined with dystrophin, P-spectrin, and vinculin.','' The 
results suggest that such sarcolemma-specific proteins are 
probably delivered to the vacuolar membranes after they 
are formed from t-tubules, most likely to reinforce the 
membrane and for exocytosis to occur. In centronuclear 
myopathy (CNM) there is an altered cytoskeletal network 
with a perinuclear binding for dystrophin, P-spectrin, and 
vimentin, and increased radial immunoreactivity for des 
min.6 Since similar features are not reported in centrally 
nucleated fibers of myotonic dystrophy and intracytoplas- 
mic dystrophin is normally found in fetal muscle, the find- 
ings strengthen the pathogenetic hypothesis of an arrested 
muscle fiber development in CNM. In central core myopa- 
thy, desmin is found reduced or absent at cores, sometimes 
with hot spots, and often markedly increased at the rim.g 
Conversely, desmin is overexpressed in target lesions in 
denervated fibers, suggesting that, despite their similarity, 
cores and targets are manifestations of different processes. 
In normal muscle fibers, desmin filaments attach to 
nuclear, mitochondrial, and inner sarcolemmal mem- 
branes and Z-bands of sarcomeres to preserve the morpho- 
logic organization of the myofiber during contraction.' 
Accordingly, altered desmin network could provide a theo- 
retical basis for explaining aggregates of mitochondria in 
LF. However, we have herein demonstrated that cytoskele- 
tal proteins network is not altered in LF. 
